---
/* DRAFT-FOR-ED — candidate node page, /next preview namespace. */
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro'
import MarkNote from '../../components/MarkNote.astro'

export const prerender = true
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Solar decision companion"
  description="A home-solar calculator built on one real house, designed to refuse easy answers."
>
  <article class="node">
    <p class="draft-stamp">draft · under review</p>
    <header data-mark="solar:masthead">
      <h1>Solar decision companion</h1>
      <p class="dek">A calculator for deciding about rooftop solar, built on one real house first.</p>
      <p class="meta">work · working · JavaScript, SVG, Playwright</p>
    </header>

    <section data-mark="solar:what">
      <h2>What it does</h2>
      <p>
        Enter a house — roof, bills, location — and it compares the real options: buy
        panels, finance them, lease, or do nothing. Every economic answer comes back as a
        range across conservative, expected, and favorable futures. Single-point answers
        aren't offered, because for a twenty-year decision they aren't real.
      </p>
      <p>
        Before its forecasts count for anything, the model must reproduce the household's
        actual electric bills within seven percent. Until it can, the interface says so.
        A candidate that fails a feasibility check — a shaded roof, an ineligible
        incentive — shows the failed check rather than hiding the option, and no amount
        of good projected return excuses it.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section data-mark="solar:how">
      <h2>How it's built</h2>
      <p>
        All arithmetic lives in one pure JavaScript module that runs identically in the
        browser and in the test suite. The finance math is cross-checked against an
        independent numerical library, and every claim-bearing word in the interface must
        trace to a computed quantity — a rule enforced by tests against a registry of
        forbidden phrasings that only ever grows. State incentive data covers
        Massachusetts in depth; other states get national figures and a pointer to the
        authoritative database, never invented numbers.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section data-mark="solar:status">
      <h2>Where it stands</h2>
      <p>
        Working, in daily use on the house it was built for, and being generalized so any
        household can load its own profile. Public release is pending; it will live at a
        subdomain of this site.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
  <MarkNote />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .node {
    max-width: var(--measure);
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: var(--space-12) var(--page-gutter) var(--space-24);
  }
  .draft-stamp {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--font-size-xs);
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-8);
  }
  h1 {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--font-size-2xl);
    line-height: var(--leading-tight);
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-3);
  }
  .dek {
    font-size: var(--font-size-md);
    font-style: italic;
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-2);
  }
  .meta {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--font-size-xs);
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-8);
  }
  h2 {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--font-size-lg);
    margin: var(--space-8) 0 var(--space-4);
  }
  p {
    line-height: var(--leading-normal);
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-4);
  }
</style>
